# 30. MOTION SYSTEM

Motion must have a purpose.

Use four categories.

## A. Micro-interactions

Fast:

- button feedback
- icon changes
- wishlist
- quantity
- toggles
- inputs
- filter selection

## B. Component transitions

Medium:

- dropdowns
- modals
- drawers
- product gallery
- mini-cart
- filter panels

## C. Page/section transitions

Controlled:

- section reveal
- hero transition
- route transition where beneficial

## D. Storytelling motion

Selective:

- campaign hero
- editorial sections
- seasonal landing pages
- brand moments

Do not use storytelling motion everywhere.

---

# 31. MOTION TIMING

Use a coherent timing system.

Suggested ranges:

- micro: 100–180ms
- standard UI: 180–300ms
- larger transitions: 300–500ms
- cinematic transitions: 500–900ms only when justified

Use smooth easing.

Avoid sluggish animations.

The user should never feel that the interface is waiting for the animation to finish.

---

# 32. SCROLL REVEALS

Use scroll-triggered reveals for:

- editorial sections
- campaign blocks
- category showcases
- featured product groups
- brand storytelling

Possible effects:

- fade + translate
- subtle scale
- masked image reveal
- staggered content
- image clipping/reveal

Keep them restrained.

Do not animate every product card independently on every scroll.

Prefer grouped reveals.

---

# 33. PARALLAX

Use subtle parallax only where it improves visual depth.

Good candidates:

- hero imagery
- editorial photography
- large campaign backgrounds

Avoid:

- heavy parallax on mobile
- excessive motion
- performance-heavy effects

Respect reduced-motion preferences.

---

# 34. PAGE TRANSITIONS

Page transitions may be used selectively.

Good:

- subtle fade
- shared-element feeling
- content crossfade

Bad:

- long black screens
- spinning loaders between every page
- forced cinematic intro
- transition delays

Navigation should feel instant.

---

